 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Df2K_1uiafe/",
 "title": "How to get anything from anyone",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that how you say something matters much more than what you say. By changing your communication style from attacking to collaborative, you can get what you want without making the other person feel defensive.",
 "key_points": [
  "What you say matters much less than how you say it.",
  "Instead of attacking someone by saying 'Jeremy, why don't you ever help me around here?', try asking nicely: 'Jeremy, could you help me with this? I'd really appreciate your help.'",
  "Instead of complaining 'Why don't you ever make time for me?', try expressing vulnerability: 'I miss you. Can we spend some time this weekend?'",
  "Instead of insulting someone's intelligence with 'How on earth do you not know how to do this? Are you dumb?', try offering support: 'Hey, which part of that is confusing? Let me help you.'",
  "People are more willing to learn and help when they do not feel stupid or attacked.",
  "You do not have to change what you want; you just need to change how you communicate it.",
  "Before asking for something, ask yourself if you are making it easy to understand or making the other person feel like they need to defend themselves."
 ],
 "content": "How to get anything from anyone(rare skill) #advice #communication I shouldn't be teaching you this but I will. Here's how you get anything from anyone. Boyfriend, girlfriend, friends, parents, or even complete strangers. There is something so ridiculously simple that most people haven't figured it out yet. What you say matters so much less than how you say it. I'm gonna prove it to you. Example one. Instead of saying, Jeremy, why don't you ever help me around here? and said you could try. Jeremy, could you help me with this? I'd really appreciate your help. Same goal completely different feeling. The first one will make someone feel attacked. The second will give them an opportunity to help, and they probably want to if you ask nice. Example two. Why don't you ever make time for me? and said you could try. I miss you. Can we spend some time this weekend? Second one's kinda cute. The first one feels like an attack and it's gonna make the other person wanna defend themselves. The other is telling them exactly what you want. Example three. How on earth do you not know how to do this? Are you dumb? and said you could try. Hey, which part of that is confusing? Let me help you. People are a lot more willing to learn when they don't feel stupid for not knowing. And here's the crazy part. You do not have to change what you wanted. You just changed how you communicated it. So the next time you need something from someone, ask yourself. Am I making it easy for this person to understand me? Or am I making them feel like they need to defend themselves? I'm Kam. You could be completely right about something, but you can still communicate it in a way that makes the other person not want to listen. I'll see you in the next one.",
 "status": "done",
